C=[]
def add(n,items,scans,lines,his,statement,pages,cl,fate,refs=(),confidence='high',calc=(),date='See checked reference metadata; assessed2026-11-19.'):
    C.append(dict(id=f'C-SA06-{n}',items=[f'SA06-{x}' for x in items],cote='14',scans=scans,lines=lines,
      his_statement=his,statement=statement,status_on_pages=pages,
      computation={'code':'parts/cote14_early/computations.py','output':'parts/cote14_early/audit/computations_output.txt','checks':list(calc),'proof':'parts/cote14_early/proof_checks.md'} if calc else {'required':'No substantive numerical computation; proof/hypothesis discussion in proof_checks.md.'},
      **{'class':cl},fate=fate,fate_date=date,present_status='As of2026-11-19: '+fate,refs=list(refs),confidence=confidence))

add(1,[6,7],[3],'Pro13–30','Une telle structure sur G_Q ... sur G_C, et réciproqt',
 'Proposed effectivity subcategory M+ is strictly full, sum/tensor/subobject closed, contains Lambda(-1), and every object becomes effective after an integral Tate twist. Equivalence of Q- and C-descriptions requires descent invariance; a complex cone without it is insufficient. The ambient M and prior axioms are not defined here.',
 'stated without proof; proposed axioms with deleted quotient and unit clauses', 'F',
 'The exact global structure cannot be identified without missing axioms. Weight-category descent is established for specified rational groups, but this does not identify the intended motive category.', ['DeligneMilne1982','Milne2004Shimura'],'high')
add(2,[9],[5],'Pro57–61','P(i) ... stable ... epsilon(i)>0',
 'For a finite-dimensional rational representation category and cocharacter i, P(i) consists of objects whose complex i-weights are nonnegative. It is closed under sums, tensor products and subobjects. If the chosen Tate character epsilon has positive i-degree m, every object enters P(i) after tensoring by epsilon^N for sufficiently large N.',
 'proof sketched: On voit', 'E','Elementary weight decomposition proves the statement. Integer twist signs are interchangeable for an existence quantifier over Z; directional effectivity uses a fixed positive Tate power.', ['Milne2004Shimura'],'high',['T01'])
add(3,[10],[5],'Pro57','ses conjugués ... et ... autom. de C',
 'If i′=g i g^-1, rho(g) identifies equal-weight spaces, hence P(i′)=P(i). Field-automorphism invariance additionally uses the rational descent of the object and the effectivity data.',
 'proof sketched: On voit', 'E','Inner-conjugation invariance is a direct intertwiner identity; rational descent is the separate Galois-grading condition in the torus model.', ['Milne2004Shimura'])
add(4,[11,14],[5,9],'Pro64–65,135–137','Prenant des intersections ... sauf peut être',
 'Intersections preserve closure under sums, subobjects and tensor products, but eventual Tate effectivity need not persist for an infinite family of cocharacters. Uniform boundedness of negative weights is necessary.',
 'open on the pages; explicit caveat', 'C','The literal unrestricted claim fails: for G_m², epsilon=x, i_m=(t,t^m), m∈Z, the character y epsilon^N has degree m+N. For every N choose m=-N-1. This example does not claim to satisfy missing polarization axioms.', ['Milne2004Shimura'],'high',['T02'])
add(5,[12],[7],'Pro90–103','invariant pr W (mais peut-être pas pr W′!)',
 'The nonnegative-weight cone attached to an inner-invariant full subcategory is Weyl invariant. Invariance under outer automorphisms of the pair(G,T) is an additional requirement.',
 'stated without proof with explicit qualification', 'E','The distinction is correct already for a split rank-two torus. The cone generated by(2,0),(1,1) is not invariant under inversion of the second character; the image(1,-1) requires a negative second cone coefficient.', ['Milne2004Shimura'],'high',['T03'])
add(6,[13],[7,13,15],'Pro92–107,236,291–297','Gamma′=Gamma/W; via un quotient fini',
 'For a connected reductive group over Q with a maximal torus, cocharacter conjugacy classes over C are X_*(T)/W. The finite-rank torus lattice has continuous Galois action through a finite quotient. A global finite quotient is not automatic for a protorus.',
 'stated without proof; missing-prime reading alarm', 'E','Finite-rank descent is classical. A finite Z-basis has open pointwise stabilizer. For an infinite product of tori split by distinct quadratic extensions, finite rank is absent and the total Galois image can be infinite.', ['DeligneMilne1982','Milne2004Shimura'])
add(7,[14,15],[9],'Pro135–137','saturation que nous ne précisons pas ici',
 'The author proposes classifying intersections of P(i) by Galois-invariant cones satisfying an unspecified saturation condition and an unspecified strict interior condition involving epsilon.',
 'open on the pages; definition expressly withheld', 'F','No exact theorem can be assigned to the undefined saturation and topology. Polar duality supplies possible reconstructions, but the monoids2N andN having equal positivity loci does not refute the normalized I(G) question.', ['Milne2004Shimura'])
add(8,[16],[9,13],'Pro141,238','i1 bar i1=j; epsilon i1=id',
 'If epsilon is rational and i1 bar(i1)=j with epsilon∘i1=id, then epsilon∘j=[2] on G_m. Thus the chosen effective Tate object has j-weight+2.',
 'proved there by displayed identities, implication implicit', 'E','Applying a character to the product proves the normalization. This fixes the sign of the weight convention without defining the missing third structure.', ['DeligneMilne1982'],'high',['T04'])
add(9,[17],[11],'Pro182–190','Cette dernière application ... injective ?',
 'The fragment asks about injectivity of either I^a(G)→Pol(G)×Eff(G) or I(G)→Eff(G), with real conjugacy and normalized polarizing cocharacters. Both nearby formulations are retained.',
 'open on the pages', 'F','The exact domain depends on absent axioms. Modern polarization/torsor results classify related structures but cannot decide either map without specifying I(G) and the equivalence relation.', ['DeligneMilne1982'])
add(10,[18],[11],'Pro188','sont elles dans Im I(G) ?',
 'Does an effectivity structure coming from motives equal P(i1) for a single admissible normalized cocharacter?',
 'open on the pages', 'F','No chosen motivic category, realization or admissibility definition is supplied. Nori and André constructions do not answer the uninstantiated one-cocharacter question.', ['DeligneMilne1982','HuberMuellerStach2017'])
add(11,[19],[11],'Pro190–192','propriété intrinsèque ... indép. du foncteur',
 'When admissibility conditions descend under inner twisting, the property that an effectivity subcategory is defined by an admissible cocharacter is invariant under changing the fibre functor.',
 'proof sketched; torsor mechanism suggested', 'C','Tannakian torsor transport proves the conditional form: local trivializations differ by conjugation and preserve weight inequalities. The missing manuscript conditions on I(G) have not thereby been checked.', ['DeligneMilne1982'])
add(12,[20],[11,13],'Pro192,232','motif sur schéma connexe S ... De-Rham',
 'Proposed extension of the four structures to motives over connected S with a rational point and a de Rham fibre functor; the subsequent Hodge/de Rham sentence is deleted.',
 'open on the pages; program, not a construction', 'F','Later motivic local systems supply specified relative categories, but do not identify the author’s four undeclared structures. The precise source proposal remains uninstantiated.', ['Arapura2013'])
add(13,[22],[11],'Pro196','propriétés arithmétiques ... éléments de Frobenius',
 'Expectation that the polarization/effectivity structures admit deeper arithmetic formulations through Frobenius elements.',
 'open on the pages', 'F','No exact quantifiers, ground field or Frobenius criterion is stated. Tori of Frobenius and Tate-class criteria are relevant developments only after those choices.', ['Milne2001Tate'])
add(14,[23],[13,15],'Pro236–240,291–300','K(T)=Z(M)^pi',
 'For a Q-torus T split by a finite Galois extension with lattice M, K0(Rep_Q(T))≅Z[M]^pi. Actual representation characters have nonnegative integral coefficients constant on Galois orbits.',
 'stated without proof', 'E','Orbit representations of a torus have one copy of each character in the orbit, yielding this ring and positive-monoid identification. This positivity is distinct from motivic effectivity.', ['Milne2004Shimura'])
add(15,[23],[15],'Pro297–300','Anneau de type fini sur Z',
 'For finite-rank M and a finite group pi of lattice automorphisms, the invariant Laurent ring Z[M]^pi is finitely generated over Z.',
 'stated without proof', 'E','The orbit polynomial makes Z[M] integral over its invariants; choose invariant coefficients for finitely many Laurent generators, obtain a finite module over a finitely generated noetherian subring, and then the invariant subring is a finite submodule. This gives a direct proof without a characteristic-zero averaging assumption.', ['Milne2004Shimura'])
add(16,[24,25],[13,15],'Pro244–250,310','J’affirme ... Ker epsilon est compact',
 'For the torus equipped with the intended polarization structure, ker(epsilon) is compact over R; its maximal split quotient has dimension1.',
 'stated without proof; attempted proof cancelled', 'C','Under polarized Tate-triple axioms, the compact-inner-form criterion gives this conclusion because a torus has no nontrivial inner automorphisms. Identification of those axioms with missing3° is conditional; the later Fausse annotation has uncertain scope.', ['DeligneMilne1982'])
add(17,[25,28],[15],'Pro310','On ne peut pas ... Q pr R',
 'The author rejects replacing Q by R in the fixed-weight finiteness question.',
 'corrected by himself later: attempted simplification rejected on scan15', 'C','Over R, T=G_m×U(1)² admits infinitely many irreducible real characters supported on the second compact factor with the same weight and chosen i1-degree0. The example does not satisfy the additional axiom that effective weight-zero objects have trivial G0-action; it only tests the proposed field simplification.', ['Milne2004Shimura'])
add(18,[26],[15],'Pro300','valeurs >=0 sur ... S(R)° ??',
 'Question whether positivity of virtual characters at positive real points characterizes effective characters or nonnegative cocharacter degrees.',
 'open on the pages; false as a literal criterion', 'C','For G_m, x^-1 is positive on R_{>0} but has negative cocharacter degree; x²-x+1 is everywhere positive there but has a negative coefficient and is not an actual representation character.', ['Milne2004Shimura'],'high',['T05','T06'])
add(19,[27],[15],'Pro300–308','fonction polynômiale ... restant finie',
 'A finite Laurent polynomial bounded as t→0 has no negative exponent. For an actual character, whose weight multiplicities are nonnegative, this is equivalent to nonnegative weights after restriction to a specified cocharacter.',
 'proof sketched by equivalence of descriptions', 'E','The term of least exponent proves the Laurent assertion; nonnegative weight multiplicities rule out cancellation. This criterion needs a chosen cocharacter and does not select one intrinsically.', ['Milne2004Shimura'],'high',['T06'])
add(20,[8,28],[3,15],'Pro27–28,310','nb fini de types ... poids i ... ???',
 'Fixed-weight finiteness is proposed with the word types undefined. Counting all isomorphism classes is false whenever a nonzero effective object exists, since its arbitrary finite direct sums retain the weight.',
 'open on the pages', 'F','A statement about irreducibles for a fixed finite-type group could be meaningfully tested, but is a reconstruction. Neither the direct-sum counterexample nor the real-torus example decides the author’s unspecified types.', ['Milne2004Shimura'])
add(21,[40,41],[19,21],'Pro359–397,423–470','valeurs successivement -2, et2',
 'For an elliptic curve E, the normalized Poincaré divisor Theta0=Delta−E×0−0×E on E×E has square degree−2. In the displayed divisor-embedding construction, the selfintersection trace is−2d, where d is the selected normal-bundle intersection degree; d=±1 gives opposite signs.',
 'proved there by an intersection calculation, with support/index reading alarms', 'B','The calculation is valid after typing the projections and normal bundle correctly. A projective-bundle completion realizes both signs. It tests an unqualified pairing, not the positive primitive form with the appropriate Lefschetz/Hodge sign.', ['Kleiman1968'],'high',['I01','I02','I03'])
add(22,[42,43,44,45],[24,25,26,27],'Pro506–514,530–569,580',
 'Les cycles alb-équi. à0 ... sous-groupe gradué',
 'Let k be algebraically closed, X smooth quasi-projective, and use smooth projective connected parameter varieties T. Let N^i(X) be the subgroup generated by Z_*(nu) with deg(nu)=0 and alb_T(nu)=0. It is graded and contains rational equivalences. The author also claims each finite sum admits one such presentation.',
 'proof sketched; addition explicitly proved with good-position choices', 'C','The product-parameter proof gives a single presentation: lift nu,nu′ to T×T′ and cross terms vanish because degrees are0. P1 generates rational equivalence. Modern correspondence formalism supplies the reconstruction on Chow groups; the integral quotient is not identified with Samuel abelian or incidence equivalence.', ['StacksMoving2026','HernandezPelaez2025'],'high',['A01','A02'])
add(23,[47],[25,28],'Pro520,600–604','N′*(X) est un idéal',
 'The image N′*(X) in CH*(X) is an ideal, for the projective-parameter definition in C-SA06-22.',
 'proof sketched; reduced to external products and pullback', 'C','For a class c on X and a generator Z_*(nu), replace Z by Z·pr_X^*c and apply the projection formula. In a Chow-correspondence formulation this proves stability. The raw-cycle moving assertion is separately recorded as C-SA06-24.', ['StacksMoving2026','Kleiman1968'])
add(24,[46],[25,27,28],'Pro522,580–602','à vérifier ... existence de Z′,nu′',
 'For a morphism f:X→Y of smooth quasi-projective varieties, pullback should carry N′*(Y) into N′*(X); the explicit cycle proof asks for rationally equivalent representatives permitting the pullback and both fibre intersections.',
 'open on the pages; self-flagged moving step', 'C','Refined pullback/Chow correspondence identities establish the natural quotient version. The opened projective moving lemma permits simultaneous finite incidence conditions; extension to the author’s raw-cycle quasi-projective formulation needs the usual refined-intersection conventions rather than a verbatim citation.', ['StacksMoving2026','Kleiman1968'])
add(25,[47],[25,28],'Pro524–527,606','f propre ... f_* ... N′',
 'Proper pushforward between smooth quasi-projective varieties preserves the subgroup generated by correspondence-images of degree-zero Albanese kernels.',
 'proof sketched: attributed to intersection formalism', 'E','Compose the defining correspondence with the graph of f. Proper pushforward and projection formula give the same generator with target Y.', ['Kleiman1968'])
add(26,[47],[25,28],'Pro529,606','L’équiv. d’Albanese implique équivalence algébrique',
 'For connected smooth projective T over an algebraically closed field, a degree-zero0cycle is algebraically trivial; its image under a correspondence is algebraically trivial.',
 'proof sketched: called trivial from definitions', 'E','Connected smooth projective parameter spaces and correspondence compatibility with algebraic equivalence give this containment. It is not equality in higher codimension.', ['Kleiman1968'])
add(27,[47],[28],'Pro608–610','mêmes propriétés formelles ... formule de projection',
 'The Chow quotient by N′ has proper pushforward, smooth-variety pullback, exterior/intersection products and projection formula. Where rational and algebraic equivalence agree, Albanese equivalence agrees with both.',
 'proof sketched; formal consequence of Proposition1.2', 'C','Given the Chow formulation verified in C-SA06-22–26, projection identities descend and rat⊂Alb⊂alg proves the last assertion. This does not imply every Weil-cohomology or motivic axiom for the quotient.', ['Kleiman1968','StacksMoving2026'])

add(28,[48,49],[29,52],'Pro616–625; K14-04:104–122','nu est alb. équiv. à0 ssi ... S_X(nu)=0',
 'For X smooth projective connected over algebraically closed k, the degree-zero Albanese-equivalence subgroup of CH0(X) equals ker(alb_X). The full quotient keeps a degree component; its degree-zero part is Alb_X(k).',
 'proof sketched; reverse direction cites a blank reference', 'E','The diagonal gives one inclusion. Correspondences preserve degree-zero Albanese kernels, proving the other; the exact missing statement is present in K14-04 scan52 Corollary1.2. The blank reference itself cannot be filled without ink evidence.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'])
add(29,[48],[29,30],'Pro621,630–632','D ... alb-équi. à0 ssi ... lin. équivalent',
 'For divisors on smooth projective X, with projective parameter spaces as in C-SA06-22, Albanese equivalence to0 equals linear equivalence to0.',
 'proof sketched using the Picard variety', 'E','A divisor correspondence defines Alb(T)→Pic0(X)red and therefore kills the parameter Albanese kernel. The reverse implication is rational⊂Albanese equivalence.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'])
add(30,[50,51],[29,30],'Pro623,645','On ignore ... identique à ... linéaire',
 'Question whether rational and Albanese equivalence agree, already for0cycles, and whether every box cycle([x]−[y])×([x′]−[y′]) is rationally trivial.',
 'open on the pages', 'B','They do not agree over C: E×E has pg=1, so Mumford’s obstruction makes its degree-zero Chow group larger than its Albanese. Box cycles generate its Albanese kernel by expanding arbitrary point pairs, hence some boxes are nonzero modulo rational equivalence. This is an existence argument, not a claimed explicitly chosen nonzero point pair.', ['Mumford1969'],'high',['A01','A02'],date='Mumford communicated1968-11-28; published1969; assessed2026-11-19.')
add(31,[52],[30],'Pro634–645','Soient z,z′ ... alg. équiv. à0 ... zz′=0',
 'The ideal P*(X) of algebraically trivial classes in CH*(X)/N′*(X) has square0. More generally, the external product of two algebraically trivial classes vanishes in the corresponding Albanese quotient.',
 'proved there by the box-cycle construction', 'C','The product-family proof is complete in the Chow formulation: the box0cycle on T×T′ has zero degree and Albanese. A2025 result places the analogous rational product in the second orthogonal filtration, but does not identify that filtration with this integral quotient.', ['PelaezReyes2025'],'high',['A01','A02'])
add(32,[53],[30],'Pro634–636','l’un ... alg. ... l’autre num. ... ??',
 'For projective X, does an algebraically trivial class times a numerically trivial class vanish modulo Albanese equivalence?',
 'open on the pages', 'C','The proven variant on scan34 replaces numerical triviality by tau-triviality. Numerical and algebraic equivalence differ in higher codimension. Searches through regular homomorphisms, incidence equivalence and standard conjectures did not locate this exact integral image-of-Albanese-kernel assertion; no novelty claim is made.', ['Kleiman1968','HernandezPelaez2025','PelaezReyes2025'])
add(33,[54,56],[31,32],'Pro654–695','d’où un hom ... u_Z',
 'For smooth projective connected X,Y over algebraically closed k and Z∈CH^i(X×Y), the action on0cycles factors through CH0(Y)/ker(deg,alb). Restriction to degree0 gives a homomorphism u_Z:Alb(Y)(k)→A^i_Alb(X).',
 'proof sketched by the defining quotient', 'C','The factorization is immediate from the definition and C-SA06-28. Modern regular homomorphisms reverse the universal direction (cycles to an abelian variety); they are not automatically the same construction.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','HernandezPelaez2025'])
add(34,[55],[31,34],'Pro670–672,749','ne dépend que ... tau',
 'The degree-zero homomorphism u_Z depends only on Z modulo tau-equivalence, meaning that some nonzero integral multiple of a difference is algebraically trivial.',
 'proved there later: postponed scan31, argument scan34', 'C','Algebraically trivial correspondences act as0 by the square-zero result. If m u=0 and Alb(Y)(k) is m-divisible, then u=0. This argument uses algebraic closedness and says nothing analogous for arbitrary k-rational points.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'],'high',['A09'])
add(35,[56,57],[32,33],'Pro681–744','on peut trouver U ... S_Y(U(a))=na',
 'For smooth projective Y over algebraically closed k, there is a positive integer n and an integral cycle U on Y×Alb(Y) inducing[n] under alb_Y. Consequently u_Z becomes an algebraic family after the isogeny[n]. An integral universalcycle inducingid is not asserted.',
 'proof sketched; existence of the multiple cycle invoked without construction', 'B','Miniversal-cycle theory supplies exactly the positive-degree construction. Voisin’s2025 counterexample rules out replacing n by1 in general, even with representable CH0. Rational equality in the Albanese quotient is distinct from equality of raw cycles.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','Voisin2025Universal0'])
add(36,[58,59],[34],'Pro751','alb-équivalent à ... Z(a0)',
 'Every algebraically trivial class in A^i_Alb(X) is the value of a group-homomorphic cycle family parametrized by an abelian variety; conversely such values are algebraically trivial.',
 'proof sketched as Corollary2.2', 'C','A smooth connected parameter family factors through its Albanese, and the multiple-cycle construction plus divisibility gives the assertion. The word diviseur must mean a codimension-i cycle unless i=1; it remains a scan alarm.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'])
add(37,[60],[34],'Pro753','P^i(X) ... n divisible',
 'For every n>0, the group P^i(X) of algebraically trivial classes in the Albanese quotient is n-divisible over an algebraically closed field.',
 'proof sketched as Corollary2.3', 'E','Algebraically trivial Chow classes are generated by curve families; Jacobian k-points are divisible, as are their images and quotients. Divisibility alone does not make P^i(X) the points of an abelian variety.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'])
add(38,[53],[34],'Pro755','l’un ... alg. ... l’autre tau ... ZZ′=0',
 'In the Albanese quotient, an algebraically trivial class times a tau-trivial class is0.',
 'proved there as Corollary2.4, proof implicit', 'C','If m w is algebraically trivial and z=m z0 with z0 algebraically trivial, then z w=z0(mw)=0 by C-SA06-31. This resolves the tau version, not the numerical version C-SA06-32.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'],'high',['A09'])
add(39,[61],[35],'Pro760–781','Si phi est définie pr un Z ... algébrique',
 'A family Y(k)→A^i_Alb(X) induced by an integral correspondence is algebraic in Definition2.5: it factors through the graded Albanese group and some positive integral multiple is induced by a cycle on X×Alb(Y).',
 'proof sketched: explicitly C’est2.1', 'C','This is the factorization plus miniversal-cycle argument C-SA06-33,35. The definition includes factorization as a separate condition, which must be preserved in later saturation statements.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'])
add(40,[61],[35],'Pro783','Si Nphi est alg., phi l’est',
 'Literal reading: among all maps Y(k)→A^i_Alb(X), algebraicity is saturated under multiplication by a nonzero integer. Refined reading: among maps already factoring through the graded Albanese group, the denominator condition is saturated.',
 'stated without proof; false as stated for unrestricted point maps', 'C','Take k=C, Y=P1, X=an elliptic curve, i=1 and t a nonzero2torsion point in Pic0(X). Set phi(infinity)=t and phi=0 elsewhere. Then2phi=0 is algebraic, whereas phi cannot factor through Alb(P1)=0. The refined ambient-group assertion C-SA06-91 follows by multiplying denominators.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'],'high',['A07'])
add(41,[61,62],[35],'Pro783–787','Fonctorialité enY ... enX ... entiers',
 'Algebraic families in Definition2.5 form a group, are contravariantly functorial in parameter Y, and are covariantly functorial under integral correspondence actions on the target cycle group.',
 'stated without proof', 'C','Common multiples combine denominator witnesses; graph/correspondence composition proves functoriality when factorization through Albanese is already imposed. Integral coefficients matter because the target can have torsion.', ['Kleiman1968'],'high',['A08'])
add(42,[62],[36],'Pro792–803','Fixons un pt y0 ... isom',
 'With y0∈Y(k), the group of algebraic families is the product of a constant value in A^i_Alb(X) and algebraic group homomorphisms Alb(Y)(k)→P^i(X).',
 'proof sketched as a direct observation', 'C','Subtract phi(y0) and use the pointed Albanese factorization. The statement depends on a chosen base point and on the corrected factorization convention; it is not a scheme-representability theorem.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'])
add(43,[62],[36],'Pro800–803','attention ... cycle à coeff rationnels quelconque',
 'A rational correspondence does not in general induce an integral group homomorphism on P^i(k), even if its codimension is correct.',
 'stated without proof, explicit warning', 'E','For an elliptic curve over C, half the diagonal would require a group map h with2h=id on E(C). A nonzero point in E[2] makes this impossible:2h(t)=h(2t)=0≠t. Passing to rationalized groups changes the question.', ['Tate1966'],'high',['A08'])
add(44,[63],[36],'Pro803','Ces homs se composent',
 'Group homomorphisms between P-groups for which a nonzero integral multiple comes from an integral correspondence are closed under composition.',
 'stated without proof', 'E','If Nf=Z_* and Mg=W_*, then MN(gf)=(W∘Z)_*. Properness and correspondence degrees are as in the smooth projective Chow setting.', ['Kleiman1968'])
add(45,[64],[37],'Pro808–810','s’ils peuvent s’inverser ... isomorphismes',
 'If an algebraic group homomorphism between the abstract P-groups is bijective, must its inverse be algebraic in the same denominator-clearing sense?',
 'open on the pages', 'C','For actual abelian varieties up to isogeny the inverse exists rationally; this does not resolve bijections between the manuscript’s potentially nonrepresentable P-groups. No exact general result was identified in the specified searches.', ['Tate1966','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','HernandezPelaez2025'])
add(46,[65],[37],'Pro812–821','Dualité ... i+j=n+1',
 'For X of dimension n and a correspondence Z∈CH^i(X×A), transposition sends P^j(X) to P^(i+j−n)(A). To land in Pic0(A)=A^vee(k), one must have i+j=n+1.',
 'proof sketched by a diagram', 'E','This is the correspondence codimension formula. It supplies a degree-correct proposed duality, not yet independence from the representative cycle.', ['Kleiman1968'],'high',['A05'])
add(47,[66],[37],'Pro821–825','il faut vérifier ... pas du cycle Z',
 'Does the transposed map P^(n+1−i)(X)→A^vee(k) depend only on the pointwise map A(k)→P^i(X), rather than on its correspondence representative? The same question is asked for general P-to-P maps.',
 'open on the pages; explicitly flagged', 'C','Algebraic representatives and distinguished autodualities establish related statements under further hypotheses, but no source checked identifies this exact quotient or proves the unrestricted transposition statement. Codimension1/top-degree cases reduce to Picard–Albanese duality.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2025','HernandezPelaez2025'])
add(48,[67,68,69],[38,39],'Pro830–878','u_Z=0 implies a(tZ) ... nul; dém. pasfaite',
 'For k algebraically closed, X smooth projective of dimension n, A an abelian variety, l≠char(k), and integral Z∈CH^i(X×A), does u_Z=0 in A^i_Alb(X) imply a(tZ):H^(2(n−i)+1)(X,Q_l)(n−i)→H^1(A,Q_l) is0?',
 'open on the pages; proof expressly not done and stops after curve reduction', 'C','Regular-homomorphism/Tate-module results give important comparisons in codimensions1,2,dimX. They do not by themselves prove the exact arbitrary-codimension implication for this integral quotient. The corrected Jacobian reduction and curve projectors are valid preliminary steps; the remaining middle component is not disposed of on the page.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2025','HernandezPelaez2025'],'medium',['A03','A04','A06'])
add(49,[73],[38],'Pro849','A-t-on une réciproque',
 'Does vanishing of the rational l-adic map in C-SA06-48 imply u_Z=0 on A(k) in the integral Albanese quotient?',
 'open on the pages', 'C','A cohomological vanishing statement loses torsion and requires a carefully specified cycle quotient. The searched regular-homomorphism literature gives no matching unrestricted integral converse; rational or representable variants must be stated separately.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','HernandezPelaez2025'])
add(50,[72],[39],'Pro876–878','la décomposition ... est algébrique',
 'For a smooth projective pointed curve C, the Künneth projectors are represented by c0×C, Delta−c0×C−C×c0, and C×c0, with order determined by correspondence convention. Tensoring with X gives the three Künneth summands with C-degrees0,1,2.',
 'proof sketched: verifies easily', 'E','The elementary curve decomposition is algebraic. It removes the two extreme cohomological components from the family-map question, but does not prove vanishing of the middle component.', ['Kleiman1968'],'high',['A06'])
add(51,[71],[38,39],'Pro863–872','C(k) ... A(k) surjectif',
 'The printed assertion that an ample curve C⊂A induces a surjection C(k)→A(k) is false when dimA>1. The induced map of Jacobians J(C)→A is surjective for a generating ample curve.',
 'false as stated; likely source/transcription compression, ink unavailable', 'C','For an abelian surface, C is a proper closed subset, so has missing k-points. J(C)→A is the precise map needed to test vanishing of a homomorphism on A(k). No reading is changed.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard'])
add(52,[70],[38,39],'Pro838,848,861,876','H^(n−i+1) ... H^1',
 'The transpose of a codimension-i correspondence on X^n×A shifts cohomological degree by2i−2n, so its degree-one target uses source2(n−i)+1, and curve Künneth terms have degrees(N,0),(N−1,1),(N−2,2).',
 'stated without proof; inconsistent indices in the transcription', 'E','Degree counting forces the indicated alternatives. These are mathematical alarms only; source images are absent and no transcription emendation has been applied.', ['Kleiman1968'],'high',['A03','A04','A05'])

add(53,[83],[41,42],'Pro893–929','les inclusions ... l’égalité des extrêmes',
 'Assuming the divisor Tate assertion and a pseudoalgebraicity or two-Frobenius-eigenspace hypothesis, the manuscript asserts that every invariant in H^(2i)(A,Q_l(i)) is generated by divisor classes. The pro-Lie algebra g(k), its Frobenius elements and pseudoalgebraicity are not defined in this fragment.',
 'proof sketched only: the equality of extreme spaces is asserted', 'F','The inclusions decomposable⊂algebraic⊂invariant are formal under the stated cycle-class convention. The asserted converse cannot be matched to an exact theorem without specifying g and its eigencharacters. Modern Lefschetz-group criteria distinguish abelian varieties with exotic Tate classes; the broad all-abelian-varieties version is not a consequence of the divisor Tate theorem.', ['Tate1966','Milne2001Tate'])
add(54,[82],[41,42],'Pro910–929','puissance ... courbe elliptique ... corps fini',
 'For an elliptic curve E over a finite field and every power E^r, all rational l-adic Tate classes over the algebraic closure are generated by divisor classes, with Tate meaning classes fixed over some finite extension and l≠p.',
 'proof sketched; divisor case attributed to Mumford, higher powers reduced to invariants', 'B','The exact special case is established. Tate1966 proves the Hom/Tate-module theorem and explicitly recalls Mumford’s elliptic observation about1962; Milne AppendixA TheoremA.3 and its elliptic example identify all Tate classes on powers as Lefschetz classes. This is not a claim about every abelian variety.', ['Tate1966','Milne2001Tate'])
add(55,[84],[41],'Pro905–909','epsilon q^(-1/2), epsilon ... racine de l’unité',
 'The bracketed description of pseudoalgebraicity says normalized Frobenius eigenvalues are roots of unity. If read as holding for every elliptic curve over a finite field, it is false; it can describe the supersingular alternative.',
 'false as stated under the unrestricted elliptic reading; alternative case retained', 'C','For E/F5:y²=x³+x+1 the discriminant is nonzero, #E(F5)=9 and trace=-3. If alpha/sqrt5 were a root of unity, ((alpha+baralpha)/sqrt5)^2=9/5 would be a rational algebraic integer, impossible. The alternative two-eigenspace hypothesis still applies to the ordinary example, so the special elliptic-power conclusion is not refuted.', ['Tate1966','Milne2001Tate'],'high',['F01','F02','F03','F04'])
add(56,[79,98],[44,45],'Pro935–945; K14-04:4–8','C^i ... mod équivalence algébrique ... isomorphisme',
 'For smooth projective connected X/k, k algebraically closed, set C^i=(cycles modulo algebraic equivalence)⊗Q. The letter conjectures L^(n−2i):C^i→C^(n−i) is an isomorphism for2i≤n.',
 'open on the pages; explicitly conjectured', 'B','False in these exact coefficients/equivalence groups. A very general complex quintic threefold has C^1=QH but a nonzero, indeed infinitely generated rational Griffiths part in C^2. The image of L on C^1 is QH², which contains no nonzero homologically trivial class because degH³=5. Clemens1983 gives the necessary rational nontriviality. Grothendieck1969 instead formulates the standard Lefschetz conjecture for algebraic cohomology classes.', ['Clemens1983','Grothendieck1969Standard'],'high',['S01','S02'])
add(57,[80,98],[45],'K14-04:12–14','la forme ... sur P^m ... définie positive',
 'For dimX=2m, the letter proposes positive definiteness of(-1)^m deg(xx′) on ker(L:C^m_alg(X)→C^(m+1)_alg(X)), where C_alg means modulo algebraic equivalence with Q coefficients.',
 'open on the pages; conjectureB', 'C','The published Hodge standard conjecture is on primitive algebraic cohomology classes, and is known over C by Hodge theory but open in general positive characteristic. A homologically trivial class disproves this draft form only if it is also nonzero modulo algebraic equivalence and primitive in that quotient; no such implication is silently assumed here. The quintic counterexample to C-SA06-56 alone does not supply an even-dimensional primitive counterexample to this statement.', ['Grothendieck1969Standard','Kleiman1968'],'medium')
add(58,[81,98],[45],'K14-04:6–8','A′ ... image directe ... surjective',
 'For a smooth hyperplane Y⊂X^n and2i≥n−1, pushforward C^i_alg(Y)_Q→C^(i+1)_alg(X)_Q is conjectured surjective. The reduction fromA is qualified by descent over nonclosed fields or by replacing algebraic with cohomological equivalence.',
 'open on the pages; reduction explicitly qualified', 'B','The literal algebraic-equivalence form is false: for a very general quintic threefold X and any smooth hyperplane surface Y, C^1(Y)_Q=NS(Y)_Q is finite-dimensional, while C^2(X)_Q contains an infinite-dimensional rational Griffiths quotient by Clemens. Thus it cannot be surjective. The cohomological refinement is part of the published standard-conjecture framework; the manuscript’s descent caveat is preserved.', ['Clemens1983','Grothendieck1969Standard','SGA6XIII1971'],'high')
add(59,[85,98],[46,47],'K14-04:20–28','A″ ... C^i(U)=0 ... 2i>n',
 'For U=X−Y a smooth affine hyperplane complement, the letter predicts cycles modulo algebraic equivalence tensorQ vanish in codimensioni with2i>dimU. It asks whether the same holds for every smooth affine variety.',
 'open on the pages; conjecture and proposed generalization', 'B','The hyperplane-complement assertion is already false for a very general quintic threefold. Localization gives C^2_alg(Y→X)_Q→C^2_alg(X)_Q→C^2_alg(U)_Q→0, where the first term is the finite-dimensional image of NS(Y)_Q; the rational Griffiths quotient onX is infinite-dimensional. This argument uses extension of algebraic families by closure to identify the quotient after localization. The cohomological vanishing H^4(U,Q_l)=0 follows from affine Lefschetz and does not force algebraic equivalence.', ['Clemens1983','SGA4XIV1973','SGA6XIII1971'],'medium')
add(60,[98],[45],'K14-04:10','A implique la formule de Künneth ... pas équivalents',
 'The letter asserts thatA implies algebraicity of the cohomological Künneth projectors, and explicitly retracts a previous assertion of equivalence.',
 'proof sketched; own earlier equivalence corrected in same letter', 'A','Grothendieck1969 §3 proves the standard implicationB(X)⇒C(X) and the equivalence of globalA andB on algebraic cohomology classes. It does not preserve the letter’s stronger modulo-algebraic-equivalenceA, nor assert C⇒B. This is the precise published successor.', ['Grothendieck1969Standard','Kleiman1968'])
add(61,[98],[45],'K14-04:10','théorèmes d’entiers ... puissances de p',
 'Algebraic Künneth projectors are claimed to give rationality/integrality and coefficient independence for characteristic polynomials of algebraic correspondences, with a possible p-denominator caveat.',
 'stated without proof; scope expressed informally', 'A','Grothendieck1969 §2pp194–195 supplies the trace/polynomial argument with denominators uniformly bounded and requires a projector represented by the same cycle for each l for l-independence. The word tous in the letter is not treated as an unrestricted integrality theorem for rational correspondences. For Frobenius, Deligne1974 Theorem1.6 gives integral l-independent polynomials unconditionally.', ['Grothendieck1969Standard','Deligne1974Weil'])
add(62,[98],[45],'K14-04:10–14','valeurs absolues ... justiciable deB',
 'The standard Lefschetz algebraicity and Hodge positivity package is expected to imply the Riemann-hypothesis part of the Weil conjectures for smooth projective varieties over finite fields.',
 'stated without proof as intended consequence', 'A','Grothendieck1969 §4p197 explicitly derives Weil fromB(X) and Hodge positivity forX×X, with the appropriate algebraic-cohomology definitions. Deligne1974 Theorem1.6 proves the Frobenius absolute values independently of the standard conjectures; lectures circulated July1973, publication1974.', ['Grothendieck1969Standard','Deligne1974Weil'])
add(63,[98],[46],'K14-04:18','catégorie ... semi-simple ... algèbre finie semi-simple',
 'Conditional onA andB and their consequences identifying the cycle relation with numerical equivalence, the category of pure motives and each rational correspondence endomorphism algebra are semisimple.',
 'stated without proof', 'B','Jannsen1992 Theorem1 establishes semisimplicity of numerical motives unconditionally, for a characteristic-zero coefficient field and an adequate relation. Passing from the draft’s algebraic-equivalence quotient to numerical motives requires the separate identification, which is false in general for the draft relation. A homological realization needs the standard-conjecture bridge; its existence is not supplied by semisimplicity alone.', ['Jannsen1992','Grothendieck1969Standard'])
add(64,[98],[46],'K14-04:18','théorie raisonnable ... jacobiennes intermédiaires',
 'The letter expectsA andB to give abelian parameter varieties for algebraically trivial cycle families and relate them to odd cohomology, with possible dependence ofB on constructing those varieties.',
 'open on the pages; tentative expectation, not a formulated theorem', 'C','Kleiman1971pp446–447 attributes to Grothendieck an unrecorded1964 construction for Picard-equivalence quotients. Modern regular-homomorphism theory proves algebraic representatives in codimensions1,2,dimX under stated field hypotheses, and ACV2025 proves certain distinguished autodualities under decomposition-of-diagonal hypotheses. Neither supplies unrestricted integral representability or positivity in every codimension.', ['Kleiman1971Finiteness','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','Kahn2021Regular','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2025'])
add(65,[85,98],[47],'K14-04:26','toute variété ... lisse ... cohomologiquement ... tau',
 'For every smooth variety, projective or not, the letter asks whether every cohomologically trivial cycle is tau-trivial, meaning algebraically trivial after multiplication by some nonzero integer.',
 'open on the pages; explicit proposed strengthening', 'B','False for very general complex quintic threefolds by the nonzero rational Griffiths group in Clemens1983. Smoothness and projectivity hold in the counterexample. This is not a reformulation for singular hypersurfaces; none is stated on these assigned pages.', ['Clemens1983'])
add(66,[88],[4,6],'Pro38–51,72–82','C1 ... C2 ... stalks',
 'On the reused English ringed-space sheets, a finite-type algebra sheaf is central separable exactly when its stalks are central separable, under the text’s local finite-presentation/projectivity conditions. The labelC1′ for globally split endomorphism algebras is distinct fromC1.',
 'proof sketched on the reused sheets; author of this ancillary text not established', 'E','Azumaya criteria are local and the canonical mapA⊗A^op→End(A) is checked on stalks; finite locally free hypotheses make the Hom comparison valid. Bass1967 Chapter3 Theorem4.1 verifies the ring criterion and attributes the theory to earlier Azumaya/Auslander–Goldman work. No inference that all Azumaya algebras are split is allowed.', ['Bass1967KTheory','StacksBrauer2026'])
add(67,[88],[6],'Pro72–78','Hom(E,E) ... central separable',
 'For a finite locally free module sheafE of everywhere positive rank, End(E) is central separable over the structure sheaf.',
 'proved there by stalk/local matrix reduction', 'E','Locally End(E)=M_r(O); the matrix-unit identity gives the Azumaya isomorphism and centerO. The positive-rank/faithfulness condition is essential. This is a split Azumaya example, not a characterization of every class.', ['Bass1967KTheory','StacksBrauer2026'])
add(68,[88],[6],'Pro78–82','A tensor B ... central separable',
 'Tensor products and opposite algebras of finite locally free central separable sheaves remain central separable; End(E)⊗End(F)≅End(E⊗F).',
 'proved there by local tensor calculations', 'E','Reordering tensor factors gives(A⊗B)⊗(A⊗B)^op≅End(A)⊗End(B)≅End(A⊗B), and positive ranks preserve faithfulness. The finite free example in the saved code is a check of conventions only; the proof uses arbitrary local ranks.', ['Bass1967KTheory'],'high',['M01'])
add(69,[88],[8],'Pro115–127','change of base ... central separable',
 'Pullback/base change of a central separable finite locally free algebra sheaf along a ringed-space morphism remains central separable.',
 'proved there using local finite presentations', 'E','Finite projectivity identifies scalar extension ofEnd withEnd of scalar extension, so the defining Azumaya isomorphism survives arbitrary base change. Bass Chapter3 verifies the corresponding ring property. No flatness assumption is required for this finite-projective identity.', ['Bass1967KTheory'])
add(70,[89],[10,12],'Pro154–173,210–228','commutant ... multiplication is an isomorphism',
 'If A⊂B are central separable algebras over a common commutative base, the commutantC=B^A is central separable and multiplicationA⊗C→B is an isomorphism; the mutual-commutant statement follows.',
 'proved there by stalk reduction and double-centralizer argument', 'E','Bass1967 Chapter3 Corollary4.3 givesA⊗B^A≅B forAzumayaA; withB Azumaya the tensor criterion gives central separability ofC. The saved2×3 matrix calculation verifies one instance but is not used as a proof of arbitrary rank.', ['Bass1967KTheory'],'high',['M02','M03'])
add(71,[86,90],[12,14,16],'Pro210–228,263–284,321–347','Brauer group ... equivalence',
 'Stable equivalenceA⊗End(E)≅B⊗End(F) defines the Brauer group of central separable sheaf algebras; tensor product gives the group law, End(E) is neutral, andA^op is inverse.',
 'proved there; assembled on reused English sheets', 'E','The tensor identities andA⊗A^op≅End(A) prove the group laws. Bass Chapter3 Proposition4.10 identifies the ring equivalence with Morita equivalence; Stacks §59.62 gives the scheme form. Exact authorship of these reused sheets is not verified by this mathematical agreement.', ['Bass1967KTheory','StacksBrauer2026'])
add(72,[87],[4,6,14],'Pro40,82,263–284','class ... zero ... C1′',
 'A central separable algebra has zero Brauer class iff it is isomorphic toEnd(P) for a finite locally free faithful moduleP. The unprimedC1 on scan4 must not be read as imposing this on every central separable algebra.',
 'proved there forC1′; likely label mismatch across sheets', 'E','Morita theory gives the splitting criterion. OverR the quaternion algebraH is central simple and has nonzero Brauer class, so the unprimed reading would be false. This is a reading alarm, not an ink correction.', ['Bass1967KTheory','StacksBrauer2026'])
add(73,[90],[14],'Pro263–284','Hom ... tensor ... isomorphism',
 'For finite locally free modulesE,F, the sheaf Hom(E,F) identifies withE^vee⊗F and the expected endomorphism tensor products commute with localization and base change.',
 'proved there by local freeness', 'E','A local basis and its dual give mutually inverse maps. These are the finite-projective hypotheses needed in the Brauer/Morita argument; arbitrary sheaf modules would not satisfy the same Hom/base-change identity.', ['Bass1967KTheory'])
add(74,[90],[16],'Pro321–347','evaluation ... isomorphism',
 'For a finite locally free faithful generatorP andA=End(P), the evaluation mapsP⊗_A Hom(P,M)→M (with module sides fixed) and their dual units give Morita equivalence.',
 'proved there by stalkwise matrix-module reduction', 'E','The dual-basis proof and matrix units establish the local inverse; the canonical maps glue. Bass1967 Chapter2 Theorem4.4 and Chapter3 Proposition4.10 give the precise categorical statement. The manuscript’s sheaf version is checked stalkwise, not inferred merely from a finite-dimensional example.', ['Bass1967KTheory','StacksBrauer2026'])
add(75,[92],[20,22],'Pro407–419,481–495','deux hyperplans parallèles ... translation',
 'The reused French reflection text uses that the product of reflections in two distinct parallel affine hyperplanes is a nonzero translation; hence a finite reflection group cannot contain both reflections.',
 'proved there on ancillary Bourbaki draft sheets', 'E','For a common unit normaln and levels c,d, r_d r_c(x)=x+2(d−c)n. Its mth power translates by2m(d−c)n and has infinite order. The saved affine-matrix computation checks the sign/order. This is independent of identifying the draft with a particular published edition.', ['BourbakiLie4562007'],'high',['R01'])
add(76,[93],[20,22],'Pro407–419,481–495','chambre ... intersection ... murs',
 'The French draft describes a chamber of a locally finite reflection-hyperplane arrangement as the intersection of the open half-spaces selected by its walls, with distinct walls nonparallel under the stated finite-group hypothesis.',
 'proof sketched on the two joined draft sheets', 'E','A component lies in a chosen side of every hyperplane; convexity of their intersection and a segment-crossing argument give the chamber description. Finiteness of the reflection group rules out parallel reflecting hyperplanes by C-SA06-75. The exact draft-number391 collation with a published Bourbaki chapter remains unverified.', ['BourbakiLie4562007'])
add(77,[99,101],[50,56],'K14-04:43–50,192–201','H^1(A_T) ... H^(2n−1)(T)',
 'For smooth projective connectedX over algebraically closedk and l≠char(k), pullback identifiesH^1(AlbX,Q_l) withH^1(X,Q_l); duality identifiesH^(2n−1)(X,Q_l)(n−1) withH^1(Pic0_redX,Q_l).',
 'stated without proof in summary; integral comparison addressed later', 'E','Picard–Albanese duality, Kummer theory and Poincaré duality give these rational identifications. Nonreduced Picard schemes in characteristicp require usingPic0_red. The summary’s degree correction onscan50 is essential; noH^(2n−1) of the Picard variety is intended.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','Kleiman1968','SGA6XIII1971'])
add(78,[100],[52,53,54],'K14-04:104–158','u_Z ... ne dépend ... numérique',
 'For smooth projective connectedX^n,Y^m over algebraically closedk andZ∈CH^n(X×Y), there is a unique homomorphismu_Z:AlbY→AlbX withalb_X(Z_*a)=u_Z(alb_Y(a)) for degree-zero0cyclesa. It depends only on the numerical class ofZ.',
 'proof sketched through a Poincaré correspondence; one reference requested', 'B','The correspondence action on Albanese follows its universal property. Numerical independence is reduced by the Poincaré divisor to numerical equivalence of divisorial correspondences: numerical-trivial divisors have torsionNéron–Severi class; the induced Hom group is torsion-free, so the homomorphism vanishes. The exact finite-generation/numerical=algebraic-up-to-torsion input is SGA6XIII4.6.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','SGA6XIII1971'],'high')
add(79,[49,100],[29,52],'Pro616–625; K14-04:120–122','S_Y(varpi)=0 ... S_X(Z(varpi))=0',
 'A correspondence acting on0cycles carries the Albanese kernel of its source into the Albanese kernel of its target.',
 'proved there asCorollary1.2 ofTheorem1.1', 'E','This follows immediately from the commutative Albanese square. It is exactly the mathematical lemma needed for the blank reference[ ,1.2.] onscan29, but matching the absent ink is not established.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard'])
add(80,[100],[52],'K14-04:122','support dansU ... A_Y(k)',
 'For every nonempty openU⊂Y, degree-zero0cycles supported onU map surjectively toAlbY(k).',
 'proof sketched in uniqueness argument', 'E','Move representatives ofCH0(Y) away fromY−U, or use a generating complete-intersection curve and divisors avoiding the finitely many excluded points. Algebraic closedness supplies the base points; this is surjectivity of degree-zero cycles, not ofU(k) itself.', ['StacksMoving2026','Grothendieck1962Picard'])
add(81,[100],[54,55],'K14-04:164–177','Theta(S_X(Z(varpi)))=c′(varpi)',
 'The Poincaré-line-bundle class associated toalb_X(Z_*a) equals the divisor-correspondence actionc′_*a inPic0(B), whereB=(AlbX)^vee. The occurrencePic0(A_X) onscan54 conflicts withscan55.',
 'proof sketched by reduction toLemma1.5', 'E','Pull the normalized Poincaré bundle toB×AlbX×Y and apply the projection formula toZ and the0cyclea. The result lives inPic0(B) by biduality; the alternative target is ill-typed. This proves the typed identity but leaves the reading to the scans.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','StacksMoving2026'])
add(82,[100],[55,56],'K14-04:181–188','les deux membres ... définis ... dispensons ... expliciter',
 'There is a divisorD linearly equivalent to the Poincaré divisor such that its intersections with the finite prescribed cycles are proper and the pushforward/intersection equality inLemma1.5 holds as cycles.',
 'stated without proof; explicitly omitted as standard intersection theory', 'E','For projective smooth varieties the simultaneous moving lemma permits the finite incidence conditions; the projection formula and associativity then give the equality. The opened Stacks lemma explicitly covers finite lists and projective ambient varieties, matching this use. No arbitrary-base relative form is claimed from it.', ['StacksMoving2026'])
add(83,[101],[56],'K14-04:190–201','Théorème1.6 ... diagramme ... commutatif',
 'TheZ_l-cohomological action of an integral correspondenceZ∈CH^n(X×Y) onH^1 is compatible with the inducedu_Z:AlbY→AlbX under the canonical Albanese pullback maps.',
 'proof sketched; follow the construction, no full argument', 'B','The cycle-class map respects the same pullback, cup product and proper pushforward used in the Poincaré construction. Thus it yields the cohomological square. The manuscript only promises the vertical maps up to finite groups; its rational summary is secure. The exact integral statement is kept separate from rational isogeny inverses.', ['Kleiman1968','Grothendieck1962Picard'])
add(84,[101],[57],'K14-04:204–206','ssi ... homomorphisme deV.A. ... indépendant ... l',
 'For abelian varietiesX,Y over algebraically closedk, an integral cycle of codimensiondimX onX×Y acts onH^1 as an actual homomorphismu:Y→X; conversely the graph ofu gives the action. For fixedZ, u is unique and independent ofl≠p.',
 'proved there asCorollary1.7', 'E','ApplyC-SA06-83 withAlbanese identities. Faithfulness of the Tate-module realization onHom implies uniqueness; the constructionu_Z occurs before choosingl, hence independence. This criterion characterizes cycle-inducedH^1 maps, not every arbitrarylinear map or everyGalois-invariant map over an arbitrary field.', ['Tate1966','Grothendieck1962Picard'])
add(85,[102],[57,58],'K14-04:208–211','base quelconqueS ... CK^n ... siA_Yexiste',
 'For smooth projective geometrically connectedX,Y over arbitraryS, a degree-n class in the gradedK-theoretic intersection groupCK^n(X×_S Y) should induceAlbY→A whenAlbY exists andX maps to anA-torsor. On geometric fibres it should agree withTheorem1.1.',
 'stated without proof; relative generalization inRemark1.8', 'C','Relative Picard/Albanese and regular-homomorphism theories establish such constructions under representability, smoothness and base-change hypotheses. The exact arbitrary-S CK-class formulation is not proved by the opened sources; the manuscript explicitly conditions existence ofAlbY. No claim of unconditional representability or universal base-change compatibility is imported.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023','SGA6XIII1971'],'medium')
add(86,[99],[49,51],'K14-04:40,55–99','quatrecas ... toutQ-hom ... Q-cycles',
 'ForA andB each selected asAlb orPic0_red of smooth projective varieties, every rational homomorphismA→B is represented in the appropriateH^1/top-odd-degree comparison by a rational algebraic correspondence. The four choices require different inverses; caseIII is integral by divisorial correspondence theory.',
 'stated without proof in the summary; case analysis outlines later proof', 'B','Picard–Albanese correspondences and the degree-one Lefschetz inverse give the rational statement. The denominator distinction is essential and is independently confirmed by modern nonexistence of integral universal0cycles. This does not assert integral inverses for arbitraryX or algebraicity of all higher-degree Lefschetz inverses.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','Kleiman1968','Voisin2025Universal0'])
add(87,[99],[50],'K14-04:50','isom ssi ... isogénie ... hom inverse',
 'A homomorphism of abelian varieties is an isogeny iff it induces an isomorphism on rationalH^1; its inverse inHom⊗Q is represented by a rational correspondence, and hence gives the inverse cohomological map.',
 'stated without proof as immediate consequence', 'E','An isogeny has an inverse up to multiplication by a nonzero integer. Conversely a rational Tate-module isomorphism forces zero-dimensional kernel and cokernel and equal dimensions. The statement is rational and does not produce an integral inverse.', ['Tate1966'])
add(88,[99],[50],'K14-04:52','zeta1 ... zeta(n−1) ... isogénie',
 'For a smooth projective connectednfold andn−1 ample divisor classes, cup productH^1→H^(2n−1)(n−1) is an isomorphism induced by an isogeny of Picard/Albanese varieties.',
 'stated without proof; promised later', 'B','The degree-one Lefschetz/Picard theory proves this mixed-ample form by a smooth complete-intersection curve after taking positive multiples of the ample divisors and by the positivity of the induced polarization. Taking multiples scales the map by a nonzero integer, harmless overQ_l. The assertion is narrower than arbitrary-degree mixed hardLefschetz in characteristicp.', ['Kleiman1968','Grothendieck1962Picard'],'medium')
add(89,[99],[51],'K14-04:88–99','il faut des dénominateurs?',
 'Questions whether integral algebraic correspondences suffice to invert the AlbaneseH^1 map and the top-odd Picard comparison; the summary proves or expects rational inverses.',
 'open on the pages; explicit denominator questions', 'C','The first inverse is equivalent, for complex smooth projectiveX, to an integral universal0cycle onAlbX×X: compatibility identifies its inducedAlbanese endomorphism withid. Voisin2025 Theorem1.4 gives counterexamples, even with representableCH0. This does not by itself decide every top-odd comparison, whose duality and lattice hypotheses must be retained.', ['Voisin2025Universal0','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2025'],'high')
add(90,[103],[58],'K14-04:217–226','u:B_X→A_Y ... unique',
 'A1cycleZ onX×Y induces a unique homomorphismPic0_red(X)→Alb(Y) sending a degree-zero divisor classD toalb_Y(pr_Y*((D×Y)·Z)) whenever the intersection is defined.',
 'stated without proof in assigned segment; proposition continues afterscan58', 'E','The displayed action is a regular group homomorphism onPic0, and the Poincaré divisor realizes it as a morphism. Its source isPic0(X) and targetAlb(Y); the margin asks about switchingX,Y but does not switch the displayed formula. The later continuation belongs to the other assigned part and must be merged at root.', ['Grothendieck1962Picard','Kleiman1971Finiteness'])
add(91,[61],[35],'Pro760–783; refined form ofC-SA06-40','condition de factorisation ... Nphi',
 'Within the group of point maps already factoring through the gradedAlbanese group, the property that some positive integral multiple of the induced homomorphism is represented by an integral correspondence is saturated under multiplication by a nonzero integer.',
 'reconstruction of a missing ambient qualification; literal assertion refuted inC-SA06-40', 'E','IfNphi has witnessm and cycleZ, thenmNphi=Z_* is a witness forphi. The factorization is an independent assumption; it cannot be recovered fromNphi when the target hasN-torsion.', ['AchterCasalainaMartinVial2023'],'high',['A07'])
add(92,[81,98],[44,45],'Pro942–945; K14-04:4–8','il suffit ... surjectivité ... n=2i+1',
 'The author proposes reducingLefschetzA to surjectivity in odd middle dimension and to the weak-cycle statementA′, with the nonclosed-field/descent caveat explicitly added in the continuation.',
 'open on the pages; verification expressly not written', 'C','Published standard-conjecture reductions operate on algebraic cohomology classes and all varieties/products; Grothendieck1969§3 provesA(X×X)⇒B(X), B⇒A and global equivalence. These do not certify the draft’s exact modulo-algebraic-equivalence reduction. Since the literalA andA′ are false, the useful remaining question is the historically qualified cohomological reformulation.', ['Grothendieck1969Standard','Kleiman1968'])
add(93,[81],[44],'Pro945; K14-04:4','dimension cohomologique ... majorée parn',
 'For an affine algebraic varietyU of dimensionn over a separably closed field, its torsion étale cohomological dimension is at mostn; smooth hyperplane sections then satisfy the weakLefschetz ranges written in the letter, for coefficients prime to the characteristic.',
 'stated as a theorem already known, contrasted with hardLefschetz', 'E','SGA4 ExpXIV Cor3.2 gives the affine bound andCor3.3 its Gysin/hyperplane consequence. Publication in1973 postdates the letter but records the earlier seminar theorem; it is not a claim that Grothendieck first proved it in1965. Deligne1980Thm4.1.1 later proves hardLefschetz.', ['SGA4XIV1973','Deligne1980Weil'])
add(94,[98],[45,46],'K14-04:14,18','tau ... numérique ... type fini ... injectif',
 'A andB are claimed to force tau-equivalence=numerical=homological equivalence, finite generation of integral tau-cycle groups, and injectivityC^i⊗Q_l→H^(2i)(i) with rank bounded byb_(2i).',
 'stated without proof as conditional consequences', 'A','Grothendieck1969§4pp198–199 formulates the standard-conjecture consequences for algebraic cohomology and numerical cycles, including finite generation and realization injectivity under stated hypotheses. The actual equality of algebraic-up-to-torsion with homological equivalence is false in general byClemens; it must not be described as an unconditional modern result. The conditional algebraic trace argument and the incorrect draft premise are distinct.', ['Grothendieck1969Standard','Clemens1983','Kleiman1968'])
add(95,[98],[46],'K14-04:18','dans l’image ... ssi ... cohomologie',
 'For a correspondence between pure motives, membership of an algebraic cycle in its image is detected by the corresponding cohomological image, conditional on semisimplicity and a faithful compatible realization.',
 'stated without proof as a consequence ofsemisimplicity', 'C','A morphism in a semisimple category splits into image andkernel, so a faithful exact realization detects the image of maps from theunit. This proves the conditional categorical statement. Numerical motives are semisimple byJannsen but no unconditional Weil-cohomology realization on them is supplied; the draft’s cycle quotient adds a further false identification.', ['Jannsen1992','Grothendieck1969Standard'])
add(96,[98],[46],'K14-04:18','semi-simplicité ... groupe deGalois ... Tate',
 'The letter expects motivic semisimplicity to imply semisimplicity of Galois actions in the Tate-conjecture setting.',
 'stated without proof; hypothesis bridge abbreviated', 'C','An exact fully faithful tensor equivalence to the relevant Galois-representation subcategory transfers semisimplicity. Semisimplicity of a motivic category or of its endomorphism algebra alone does not imply semisimplicity of the underlying Galois module; the Tate/fullness hypothesis and realization comparison are necessary. Tate1966 supplies the abelian-variety finite-field comparison, not the general assertion.', ['Jannsen1992','Tate1966'])

add(97,[65,66],[37],'Pro812–825','correspondance biunivoque entre Hom alg',
 'For dual abelian varieties A and B=A^vee, the page announces a bijection between algebraic group homomorphisms A(k)→P^i(X) and P^(n+1−i)(X)→B(k), with algebraicity defined by an integral multiple represented by a cycle.',
 'stated without proof; the integral representative argument and its independence are immediately qualified', 'C',
 'For an integral correspondence from an abelian source, transposition independence follows from the seesaw argument in proof_checks.md §8. This does not automatically divide the transposed map by the integer used in the definition of an algebraic homomorphism. Saito1979 Theorems4.4,4.6 prove duality and rational correspondence realization for the different incidence quotient. The full integral bijection for this manuscript quotient is not identified with that result.',
 ['Saito1979Intermediate','AchterCasalainaMartinVial2025'],'medium')
add(98,[91,93],[22],'Pro481–483','u longrightarrow h u est injective',
 'For affine spaces with chosen origins a,a′, an affine map u with linear part v(u) induces the linear map (t,lambda)↦(v(u)t+lambda(u(a)−a′),lambda) on homogenized vector spaces. This construction is injective on affine maps.',
 'stated without proof on the reused French sheet', 'E',
 'Restriction to the affine slice lambda=1 recovers u. The displayed formula is the standard affine-to-linear homogenization; the finite-dimensional symbolic check also verifies composition. Earlier definitions of the notation are outside the supplied leaf.',
 ['BourbakiLie4562007'],'high',['R02'])
add(99,[91,93],[22],'Pro485–487','pseudo-réflexion ... v s ... h s',
 'An affine map fixing an affine hyperplane pointwise has a linear part fixing its direction pointwise, and its homogenization fixes the homogenized hyperplane. With the sheet’s convention, being a nonidentity involution is equivalent for the affine map, its linear part, and its homogenization.',
 'proof sketched by choosing an origin in the fixed hyperplane', 'E',
 'Taking an origin on the fixed hyperplane removes the translation term. The three maps become s=v(s) and h(s)=diag(v(s),1), which proves both fixed-hyperplane and involution assertions. The general word pseudoreflection is retained with the sheet’s convention rather than supplied from an absent earlier definition.',
 ['BourbakiLie4562007'],'high',['R02'])
add(100,[91,92],[22],'Pro489','les réflexions ... déplacements ... symétries orthogonales',
 'Over a field of characteristic different from2, for a nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form, an affine involution fixing a hyperplane pointwise is an isometry exactly when it is the orthogonal reflection in a nonisotropic hyperplane.',
 'stated without proof, referring to an earlier Bourbaki proposition', 'E',
 'For an orthogonal nonidentity involution, the plus and minus eigenspaces are orthogonal; the plus space is the hyperplane and the minus space is a nonisotropic line. Conversely negating this orthogonal line preserves the form. Translating the origin to the hyperplane gives the affine assertion.',
 ['BourbakiLie4562007'])
add(101,[99,100,103],[49,52,58],'K14-04:40,104–122,217–226','ne dépendant que ... équivalence numérique',
 'In all four choices of Picard/Albanese source and target in the Sorites summary, the correspondence-induced homomorphism depends only on the numerical equivalence class of the cycle.',
 'stated without proof in the summary; the Albanese-to-Albanese case is proved in the assigned continuation', 'B',
 'The Albanese case is C-SA06-78. Fixed rational Picard–Albanese comparison correspondences and transposition reduce the other three cases to it; numerical equivalence is compatible with composition. A rationally zero homomorphism of abelian varieties is zero since Hom is torsion-free. This proves the qualified classical reconstruction, without claiming the missing continuation was read here.',
 ['Grothendieck1962Picard','SGA6XIII1971','Kleiman1968','Saito1979Intermediate'],'medium')
add(102,[69],[38],'Pro840','mod. torsion (i.e. à coefficients Q_l!)',
 'For a homomorphism f between finitely generated Z_l-modules, its rationalization is zero exactly when its image is torsion, equivalently when the induced map on the torsion-free quotients is zero. The quotients themselves are not equal to their Q_l tensor products.',
 'stated without proof as a parenthetical coefficient convention', 'E',
 'Localization kills exactly elements annihilated by a power of l; finite generation bounds that torsion exponent. Thus the vanishing tests agree. Z_l is strictly contained in Q_l, so the parenthesis cannot be read as an identification of lattices.',
 ['Tate1966'])
add(103,[88],[14,12],'Pro271–284,202–207','M finitely generated projective ... Hom(M,M) is separable',
 'For a commutative ring A and a finitely generated projective A-module M, End_A(M) is separable over A. Faithfulness of M is not required for separability, but it is required for the central faithful Azumaya statement C-SA06-67.',
 'proved there as LemmaIII.3, split across reused leaves14→12', 'E',
 'Locally M is free, possibly of rank zero. For positive rank matrix algebras the element sum_i E_(i1)⊗E_(1i) is a separability element; on rank-zero components the zero algebra is a projective module over its enveloping algebra. The defining finite-projective local criterion glues. No claim that a nonfaithful M has centerA is made.',
 ['Bass1967KTheory'],'high',['M04'])
add(104,[88],[14],'Pro270','locally projective ... over L ... over A',
 'If L is a locally separable A-algebra and M is locally projective of finite type as an L-module, then M is locally projective of finite type as an A-module.',
 'stated without proof as clear', 'E',
 'Locally M is a direct summand of a finite power of L. The Azumaya hypotheses make L finite projective over A, and direct summands of finite projective modules remain finite projective. This is projectivity transitivity under the exact hypotheses used on the page.',
 ['Bass1967KTheory'])
add(105,[89],[10,8],'Pro171–173,115–119','Hom_(Lambda^e)(Lambda,M) ... M^Lambda',
 'For a ring Lambda over its commutative center A and a Lambda-bimodule M, evaluation at1 identifies Hom_(Lambda⊗_A Lambda^op)(Lambda,M) with the centralizing elements {m: xm=mx for everyx inLambda}. The analogous sheaf identity defines the commutant.',
 'stated without proof as recalled background', 'E',
 'A bimodule map has f(x)=x f(1)=f(1)x. Conversely a centralizing m defines f_m(x)=xm, and the formulas are inverse and commute with restriction. No finiteness assumption is needed for this elementary identification.',
 ['Bass1967KTheory'])

